### Variations

**Variation 1: Eager Loading with Startup Preload**

**When to Use**: Limited number of tenants (5-10), all extensions relatively small (<10MB each), predictable tenant activity

**Approach**: Load all extensions for all active tenants during application startup

```csharp
public async Task PreloadAllTenantsAsync()
{
    var activeTenants = await _tenantService.GetActiveTenantsAsync();
    foreach (var tenant in activeTenants)
    {
        await LoadTenantExtensionsAsync(tenant.Id);
    }
}
```

**Trade-offs**:
- ✅ Zero cold-start latency for first requests
- ✅ Simpler implementation
- ❌ Higher memory consumption at startup
- ❌ Slower application startup time

**Variation 2: Per-Request Loading (No Caching)**

**When to Use**: Extensions are stateless, extremely lightweight, or change very frequently

**Approach**: Load and instantiate extensions fresh for each request

**Trade-offs**:
- ✅ Always uses latest extension version
- ✅ Minimal memory footprint
- ✅ No cache invalidation complexity
- ❌ Higher CPU overhead per request
- ❌ Increased latency (20-100ms per request)

**Variation 3: Shared AssemblyLoadContext**

**When to Use**: Extensions have compatible dependencies, isolation not critical, memory constrained

**Approach**: Load all extensions in default AssemblyLoadContext

```csharp
public IExtension LoadExtension(ExtensionDescriptor descriptor)
{
    // Loads into default context, shared across all tenants
    var assembly = Assembly.LoadFrom(descriptor.AssemblyPath);
    return CreateInstance(assembly, descriptor.TypeName);
}
```

**Trade-offs**:
- ✅ Lower memory overhead (single assembly copy)
- ✅ Better performance (no context switching)
- ❌ No dependency isolation
- ❌ Cannot unload extensions individually
- ❌ Weaker security boundaries

**Variation 4: Distributed Cache for Extension Instances**

**When to Use**: Multi-node deployments, container environments with frequent scaling, extensions are serializable

**Approach**: Cache extension instances in Redis with serialization

```csharp
public async Task<T> GetCachedExtensionAsync<T>(string tenantId, string extensionId)
{
    var key = $"{tenantId}:{extensionId}";
    var serialized = await _redis.StringGetAsync(key);

    if (serialized.HasValue)
        return JsonSerializer.Deserialize<T>(serialized);

    var extension = await LoadAndInstantiateAsync<T>(tenantId, extensionId);
    await _redis.StringSetAsync(key, JsonSerializer.Serialize(extension),
        TimeSpan.FromMinutes(30));
    return extension;
}
```

**Trade-offs**:
- ✅ Cache shared across all application instances
- ✅ Reduced memory per instance
- ✅ Faster scaling (new instances use shared cache)
- ❌ Serialization overhead
- ❌ Extensions must be serializable
- ❌ Network latency on cache access

**Variation 5: Tiered Loading Strategy**

**When to Use**: Large number of tenants with varying usage patterns

**Approach**: Combine multiple strategies based on tenant characteristics

- **Tier 1 (High-Traffic)**: Preload at startup, keep in memory
- **Tier 2 (Medium-Traffic)**: Load on first request, aggressive caching
- **Tier 3 (Low-Traffic)**: Load per request, no caching
- **Tier 4 (Inactive)**: Don't load until activity detected

```csharp
public async Task<IExtension> GetExtensionAsync(string tenantId, string extensionId)
{
    var tier = await _tenantService.GetTenantTierAsync(tenantId);

    return tier switch
    {
        TenantTier.High => await GetPreloadedExtensionAsync(tenantId, extensionId),
        TenantTier.Medium => await GetCachedExtensionAsync(tenantId, extensionId),
        TenantTier.Low => await LoadFreshExtensionAsync(tenantId, extensionId),
        _ => throw new InvalidOperationException()
    };
}
```

**Trade-offs**:
- ✅ Optimized for diverse tenant profiles
- ✅ Efficient resource utilization
- ❌ Complex implementation and configuration
- ❌ Requires tenant classification logic

**Variation 6: Lazy Assembly Loading with Eager Type Discovery**

**When to Use**: Extensions have known types/interfaces but heavy dependencies

**Approach**: Scan assemblies for types at startup, but load assemblies lazily

```csharp
public void DiscoverExtensionTypes()
{
    // Startup: use MetadataLoadContext to read types without loading assemblies
    var mlc = new MetadataLoadContext(new PathAssemblyResolver(extensionPaths));
    foreach (var path in extensionPaths)
    {
        var assembly = mlc.LoadFromAssemblyPath(path);
        var types = assembly.GetTypes().Where(t => t.Implements<IExtension>());
        _typeRegistry.Register(types);
    }
}

public IExtension LoadLazily(string extensionId)
{
    var typeInfo = _typeRegistry.Get(extensionId);
    // Now load the actual assembly
    var assembly = Assembly.LoadFrom(typeInfo.AssemblyPath);
    return CreateInstance(assembly, typeInfo.TypeName);
}
```

**Trade-offs**:
- ✅ Fast extension discovery at startup
- ✅ Delayed assembly loading reduces startup memory
- ✅ Type information available for validation
- ❌ Additional complexity with MetadataLoadContext

